The first issue has a direct contradiction, independently of any smooth-number estimate. Let \(\xi\) be the endpoint nearer zero of an ordinary block. Its defining inequalities give
\[
 \tfrac12\sqrt h\,|\xi|\le |I|\le2z,
 \qquad |\xi|\le4z/\sqrt h.
\]
Thus every ordinary block lies in \(|x|\le6z/\sqrt h\). Since \(z=h\ell\le hW\), PG(d) gives
\[
 \nu(\text{ordinary blocks})
 \le \frac{3}{W}\left(\frac{12z}{\sqrt h}+1\right)
 \le36\sqrt h+\frac3W.
\]
Outside the negligible cutoff region, the **non-ordinary** mass therefore tends to one. BL instead bounds it by \(Cd\sqrt h\to0\).

There is a clean repair: every retained block has
\[
 h_*|\xi|\le |I|\le\sqrt h\,|\xi|,
 \qquad h_*\ge y^{-5/12}.
\]
FO therefore applies throughout, including blocks labelled non-ordinary. Obtain
\[
 \Psi_i/c_i=Y_i\tau_i(1+O(E_{\rm FO}+\text{drift}))
\]
on all retained blocks. The sixteenth moments of \(Y_i,\tau_i\) then give the eighth moment by Hölder. This removes the unsupported step that turns an \(e^{O(\lambda)}\) pointwise bound into a constant eighth moment. With this repair, I see no necessary linear first-order term in the principal covariance.

For the parent FAM repair, write
\[
 R_{\rm typ}:=\widehat\rho_J(\mathfrak M V_1),
 \qquad \rho_a(q):=\widehat\rho_{B_2}(X_F/q).
\]
What is needed is the explicit package
\[
 E_e[R_1R_2]\le\Xi_pD^C R_{\rm typ}^2,\qquad
 c_i(g)\le C\rho_a(q),\qquad
 z_{\mathfrak q,e}\ge
 n_pR_{\rm typ}\rho_a(q)D^{-C}.
\]
These yield
\[
 \frac{E_e[R_1R_2c_1c_2]}{z_{\mathfrak q,e}^2}
 \le\frac{\Xi_pD^C}{n_p^2}.
\]
The cited SPR’s \(S_{\rm typ}\) already contains an algebraic factor. Importing that definition into the first inequality loses \(\rho_a(q)^2\). Its inverse has logarithm of order \(D^{3-7\alpha}\lambda\), exceeding the allowed parent loss \(D^{\theta_p}\lambda^2\).

Rerun absolute PUB with rational weights only. Prove the aggregate lower bound using active points; no individual lower bound is available for inactive points. The same-\(\ell\) contribution must likewise be stated against \(R_{\rm typ}^2\). Its proposed \((\log Y)^2/Y\) loss is plausible and sufficient, but EXC(b), as cited, is not itself the required pointwise rational lemma. Also allow ten partners for an **absolute** determinant: MULT’s five is for a signed determinant. This only changes constants.

For the root-conditioned subtraction, because \(q\nmid G(P)b\), variation of one coefficient makes \(F(P)\) equidistributed modulo \(q^2\), up to negligible endpoint errors. Hence
\[
 \Pr(q\mid F(P),q^2\mid F(P))\sim q^{-2},
 \qquad
 \Pr(q^2\mid F(P)\mid R_{\mathfrak q})\sim q^{-1}.
\]
Separate \(p=q\) from \(p\ne q\). For discriminant exclusions at \(p\ne q\), include **both** \(p\mid F(P)\) and \(p\mid f_d\operatorname{disc}f\). For \(p=q\), use the root-conditioned degeneracy estimate \(O(d/q)\). These corrections still give a total conditional subtraction \(D^C/B\). Thus the sufficient endpoint condition remains \(a>2c_2\); the erroneous inequalities do not justify weakening it.

The fibre repair also appears affordable. Cover each dyadic piece by an interval of length at least \(\lambda_{\min}\), at a comparable positive scale. There are \(O(\log X_2)\) pieces per fibre. The added normalized contribution is bounded by
\[
 D^C(d+1)^2y^{-C_X}c_1,
\]
using the area lower bound; the thinness cancels against the \(1/\vartheta\) in \(\lambda_{\min}\). Choose \(C_X\) explicitly to make this negligible relative to \(c_1c_2\). Then verify \(\lambda_{\min}/\Delta'\gg2y\) and the BTS gcd condition on each covering interval. These comparisons have sufficient margins even for thin child pairs.

The following parts survive the audit:

- **ML for smoothness events.** TP-q’s hypotheses hold: primitivity and non-proportionality, \(q\nmid b_iG_iN\), the filters, and the multiplier bound. Dividing the lattice gives modulus \(\Delta/q\), including when \(q\mid\Delta'\). The mixture and the \(21qN^{-\eta_0/2}\) covariance comparison are valid. Parents require the smoothness/acceptability distinction above.
- **PG and the main boundary geometry.** The width and chord estimates are consistent with MM. Euclidean versus maximum norms have the correct inequality direction. The extent/width factor \(O(d)\) is retained in anti-concentration. The core is convex, and an \(O(h)|\Pi|\) boundary cover and controlled convex enlargement are available. Thinness does not invalidate PG’s lattice remainders.
- **CL and CF.** The bijection is exact, including full prime-power classes. The counting remainders are negligible at the retained lengths. Singles carry no character discrepancy. Define normalized energies as zero when their numerator sequence vanishes, and discard non-\(y\)-smooth \(\delta\) before identifying sequences with library differences.
- **FO and LF-A.** The coprimality set is appropriate. The mid-prime sum tends to zero; the parent extension has \(S_0^p=y^{o(1)}\), and its Rankin tails have strict margins. The \(\beta\)-drift is sufficiently small. The local factor has exactly mean one, including prime powers and primes \(>y\), and its stated second and fixed higher moments are credible. Hildebrand’s interval range used here agrees with the authors’ [survey, Theorem 5.1](https://jtnb.centre-mersenne.org/item/JTNB_1993__5_2_411_0.pdf).
- **EN-A.** The L¹-to-L² argument matches [Drappeau’s Lemme 5](https://drappeau.perso.math.cnrs.fr/documents/bffi-friable.pdf): \(Q\le x\), permitted conductors, and \((\log x)^{c_D}\le y\le x\). Both applications have \(Q=x^{o(1)}\). The paper explicitly attributes the lemma to Harper §3.3.
- **Child FAM-A.** After the moment/library repairs, the integrated rational bound, filter spread, per-point CLB normalization, and determinant multiplicity give the required domination. The decisive summation is \(\sum_\delta\delta^{-2+2\gamma}=O(1)\). The inequality \(\max_k\sum_{\mathfrak q}\le\sum_{\mathfrak q}\max_k\) is legitimate here because the normalized domination is available label by label. Merely observing that \(k\) does not contain \(q\) would not establish it.
- **Thin pairs, skew labels, and thresholds.** The per-point envelope avoids the false max/typical comparison for skew labels. DOM and LS then suffice. Separate parent thresholds are essential and correctly chosen: \(\theta_p<3-7\alpha\), including at \(3/8\).

For PD-A, the atom construction and interior marginal domination are correct. Issue 5 is repaired by performing the boundary Hölder estimates on the joint space \((y,x)\), using the averaged fourth moments there. No independence between the mixture, gcd class, and archimedean factor is needed.

The assembly has no additional exponent obstruction that I found. The common \(T_0\) agrees with c12rat’s stated RAT range. At \(3/8\), both budget ratios are \(D^{-3/8}\lambda^C\); \(a>2c_2\) implies (SQ); the U′ range, Lemma η, and parent-value recursion have the listed margins. The parent one-point identity \(w/z=\Pr(R,e)/\#J\) is the correct normalization.

For the active proof route, I found no direct use of Harper’s Theorems 1–2 beyond Drappeau’s stated input. The dependency claim is reasonable relative to the accepted internal chain. The numerical \(C_\gamma=0\) examples remain illustrations, not certified running-time constants.

